{
 "id": 86,
 "number": 286,
 "title": "Walls and Gates",
 "slug": "walls-and-gates",
 "category": "graphs",
 "difficulty": "Medium",
 "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/walls-and-gates/",
 "pattern": "Multi-source BFS from every gate",
 "statement": "A grid `rooms` contains `-1` (a wall), `0` (a gate) and `2147483647` (INF, an empty room). Fill each empty room with the number of steps to its **nearest gate**. Rooms that cannot reach a gate stay INF. Modify the grid in place.",
 "example": {
  "input": "rooms = [[INF,-1,0,INF],[INF,INF,INF,-1],[INF,-1,INF,-1],[0,-1,INF,INF]]",
  "output": "[[3,-1,0,1],[2,2,1,-1],[1,-1,2,-1],[0,-1,3,4]]",
  "why": "Every room now holds its distance to the closer of the two gates."
 },
 "more": [
  {
   "input": "rooms = [[-1]]",
   "output": "[[-1]]"
  },
  {
   "input": "rooms = [[2147483647]]",
   "output": "[[2147483647]]"
  },
  {
   "input": "rooms = [[0,2147483647,-1,2147483647]]",
   "output": "[[0,1,-1,2147483647]]"
  },
  {
   "input": "rooms = [[0,2147483647,2147483647,0]]",
   "output": "[[0,1,1,0]]"
  }
 ],
 "idea": [
  "Running a separate search from every room (or from every gate) repeats work. Instead start a **single BFS from all gates at once**.",
  "BFS moves outward in rings: first all rooms 1 step from *some* gate, then all rooms 2 steps away, and so on.",
  "So the **first** time the search reaches a room, it has arrived from the nearest gate by a shortest route. Write the distance there; a room that already has a number is never touched again."
 ],
 "code": "class Solution:\n    def wallsAndGates(self, rooms: List[List[int]]) -> None:\n        INF = 2147483647\n        rows, cols = len(rooms), len(rooms[0])\n        queue = deque()\n\n        for r in range(rows):\n            for c in range(cols):\n                if rooms[r][c] == 0:\n                    queue.append((r, c))  # every gate is a starting point\n\n        while queue:\n            row, col = queue.popleft()\n            for dr, dc in ((1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1)):\n                nr, nc = row + dr, col + dc\n                if 0 <= nr < rows and 0 <= nc < cols and rooms[nr][nc] == INF:\n                    rooms[nr][nc] = rooms[row][col] + 1\n                    queue.append((nr, nc))",
 "complexity": {
  "time": "O(rows × cols)",
  "timeWhy": "Every cell enters the queue at most once.",
  "space": "O(rows × cols)",
  "spaceWhy": "The queue can hold a large part of the grid."
 },
